---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const vecCfg = { type: 'vector', unit: '', x: { min: -8, max: 8 }, y: { min: -8, max: 8 }, start: [5, 3] };

const rotCfg = {
  title: 'One matrix, two turns: composing rotations',
  steps: [
    { math: 'R = <sup>[ 0 −1 ; 1 0 ]</sup>', note: 'the quarter-turn matrix: columns are the images of the basis arrows e₁ = (1,0) and e₂ = (0,1).' },
    { math: 'R (1, 0) = (0·1 − 1·0, 1·1 + 0·0) = (0, 1)', note: 'east becomes north — exactly what a 90° rotation should do.' },
    { math: 'R (0, 1) = (0·0 − 1·1, 1·0 + 0·1) = (−1, 0)', note: 'north becomes west. The whole plane is now determined.' },
    { math: 'R (3, 2) = (0·3 − 1·2, 1·3 + 0·2) = (−2, 3)', note: 'a linear map is fixed by what it does to basis vectors — everything else follows.' },
    { math: 'R² (1, 0) = R (0, 1) = (−1, 0)', note: 'applying the quarter-turn twice is a half-turn. Matrix multiplication R·R encodes composition of motions.' },
    { math: 'R⁴ = I, so R is invertible: R⁻¹ = R³', note: 'four quarter-turns return every point home. Invertibility is geometric reversibility.' },
  ],
};

const vmOpSort = {
  "prompt": "File each operation under its physical meaning:",
  "buckets": [
    "addition — walking",
    "scalar multiplication — stretching",
    "subtraction — the connector"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "(3, 4) + (1, −2) = (4, 2) — the triangle law",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "2v = (6, 8) — same direction, twice the size",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "B − A is the vector that takes you from A to B",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "−v flips the arrow through the origin",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "Each operation has a physical meaning worth keeping in view — combining winds, stretching forces, finding displacements."
};

const vmPointJudge = {
  "statement": "Adding a point to a point is meaningless while subtracting two points is perfectly fine — the difference of two locations is a genuine displacement.",
  "answer": true,
  "why": "A vector is a displacement with no fixed home; drawing it from the origin is a convention, not a property."
};

const vmMagEst = {
  "prompt": "|v| = √(x² + y²). For v = (3, 4) it is √25 = 5. What is |u| for u = (1, −2)?",
  "unit": "",
  "answer": 2.24,
  "factor": 1.1,
  "why": "√(1 + 4) = √5 ≈ 2.24 — and dividing by it gives the unit vector, the way to express 'direction only'.",
  "context": "a force of 50 N along (3, 4) decomposes as 50 × (3/5, 4/5) = (30, 40) N"
};

const vmDotCloze = {
  "text": "The dot product converts two vectors into one number that measures alignment: positive means an %%0%% angle between them, negative an obtuse angle, and exactly zero means %%1%% — test (1, 2) against (−2, 1): −2 + 2 = 0.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "acute"
    ],
    [
      "perpendicular",
      "right angles",
      "at right angles"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "One sign means 'less than 90°'; zero means the other measuring instrument's favourite answer."
};

const vmCollinearSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these claims about the collinearity proof is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the other.",
    "P = (1, 1), Q = (3, 2), R = (7, 4).",
    "PQ = (2, 1); QR = (4, 2) = 2(2, 1).",
    "Since QR = 2·PQ, all three lie on one line — no need to check they share a point."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "You must also check they share the point Q — parallel displacements alone do not force collinearity.",
  "why": "Since QR = 2·PQ and they share the point Q, all three lie on one line."
};

const vmDiagCompare = {
  "prompt": "Proving the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "diagram",
    "text": "draw the parallelogram and eyeball the diagonals — works only in the plane"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "components",
    "text": "both midpoints compute to ½(A + B) — four symbols, no diagram required"
  },
  "why": "The component proof works for parallelograms in 3D or 10D just as well — dimension-independence is the quiet superpower."
};

const vmMatrixMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each linear transformation with its 2×2 matrix:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "rotation by 90° anticlockwise",
      "right": "[ 0 −1 ; 1 0 ]"
    },
    {
      "left": "reflection in the x-axis",
      "right": "[ 1 0 ; 0 −1 ]"
    },
    {
      "left": "enlargement by scale factor k",
      "right": "[ k 0 ; 0 k ]"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The columns are the images of the basis vectors — once known, the image of any vector follows by one multiplication, because M(3, 2) = 3·M(e₁) + 2·M(e₂)."
};

const vmOrderBranch = {
  "root": "Rotate the plane 90° then reflect in the x-axis, versus reflect then rotate — applied to (1, 0). Do they agree?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Trace both orders:",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Yes — both end at (0, −1)",
          "next": "no",
          "feedback": "No: rotation then reflection gives (0, −1); reflection then rotation gives (0, 1) — the matrices genuinely differ."
        },
        {
          "label": "No — order is sacred because multiplication is composition of motions",
          "next": "right",
          "feedback": "Right — and geometry told us so before the algebra did."
        },
        {
          "label": "Yes, because matrix multiplication is associative",
          "next": "assoc",
          "feedback": "Associativity lets you regroup brackets: (AB)C = A(BC); it says nothing about swapping order."
        }
      ]
    },
    "no": {
      "text": "Almost no matrices commute — MN ≠ NM in general."
    },
    "right": {
      "text": "The exception worth remembering: a matrix commutes with its own powers, and R⁴ = I for the quarter-turn."
    },
    "assoc": {
      "text": "Associative ≠ commutative — a classic conflation."
    }
  },
  "why": "Multiplication is composition of motions and motions do not commute."
};

const vmDetPlace = {
  "prompt": "For M = [ a b ; c d ], det M = ad − bc. A reflection has det = −1. What is det M for M = [ 3 1 ; 1 2 ]?",
  "labels": [
    "5",
    "6",
    "−1",
    "9"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": -2,
    "max": 10
  },
  "answer": 5,
  "tolerance": 0.4,
  "why": "6 − 1 = 5 — any shape's area is multiplied by 5, and det = 0 would mean the plane crushed onto a single line, with no inverse."
};

const vmSolveOrder = {
  "prompt": "Rebuild the inverse-matrix solution — what comes first:",
  "items": [
    "Write M = [ 3 1 ; 1 2 ] and compute det M = 5",
    "Form M⁻¹ = (1/5)[ 2 −1 ; −1 3 ]",
    "Compute x = M⁻¹b = (1/5)[ 10 ; 5 ] = (2, 1)",
    "Check: 3(2) + 1 = 7 ✓ and 2 + 2(1) = 4 ✓"
  ],
  "why": "The matrix view finds the crossing point by un-transforming the output — and det = 5 promised the lines were not parallel."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/vectors-and-matrices">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Mathematics lesson. It assumes the foundational lessons on linear
    functions and Euclidean geometry, and the intermediate lessons on trigonometry and circles
    and coordinate geometry — Pythagoras, similar triangles and coordinates are the working
    tools throughout. The physics arrows you have met are about to become lists of numbers that
    any field can use.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>add, subtract and scale vectors component-wise, and say what each operation means geometrically;</li>
    <li>compute magnitudes, unit vectors and dot products, and read alignment from the dot product's sign;</li>
    <li>prove geometry facts — midpoints, collinearity, the parallelogram law — with component arithmetic;</li>
    <li>build a transformation matrix from the images of the basis vectors, and compose transformations by multiplying matrices;</li>
    <li>use the determinant as the area scale factor and the invertibility test, and solve systems with the inverse.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    By now you have met vectors twice: as physics arrows (displacement, velocity, force) and as
    coordinates on graphs. The advanced view is the one that makes linear algebra the most-used
    mathematics in modern work: <strong>a vector is simply a list of numbers, and anything you
    can do to a list you can do to a vector</strong>. Five sensor readings, a paragraph turned
    into word counts, a portfolio of share holdings — all vectors. The questions that matter
    ("how big is it?", "how aligned are these two?", "what happens if I transform it?") then
    have answers that work identically in every field. A <strong>matrix</strong> is the next
    step up: not a list but a grid, and specifically a <em>machine</em> that eats vectors and
    outputs vectors, always of the same kind — rotated, stretched, reflected or sheared, but
    never bent. That restriction to straight-line motion is what makes matrices computable
    with four numbers (in 2D) and why the syllabus unit on transformations and vectors
    (E7.1–E7.4) is really one subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page treats the two in the order the syllabus lists them: vectors first as objects
    (arithmetic, magnitude, geometry), then transformations as the verbs that act on them,
    and finally the matrix algebra that packages those verbs so they can be multiplied,
    inverted and composed.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="one vector, many uses — its components [x, y] are just a 2-entry list that matrices act on" widget="diagramBuilder" config={vecCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag the arrow around the plane and watch the component
    pair follow it — the pair is the vector's entire data, and the drawing is only a convention.
    Keep the length fixed and rotate: the components trade off against each other. Everything
    this page computes — magnitude, dot products, matrices — reads those two numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>Vectors in two dimensions (E7.2)</h2>
  <p>
    Write a vector as a column of its components: v = (3, 4) means "3 right, 4 up". The
    arithmetic is component-wise, and each operation has a physical meaning worth keeping in
    view:
  </p>
  <Widget no="12" caption="file each operation under its physical meaning" widget="sort-buckets" config={vmOpSort} />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Addition is walking.</strong> (3, 4) + (1, −2) = (4, 2): go right 3 and up 4, then right 1 and down 2; net right 4, up 2. This is the triangle law — the sum is the third side of the triangle formed by the two arrows laid end to end. Physically it is how you combine a plane's air velocity with the wind velocity to get ground velocity.</li>
    <li><strong>Scalar multiplication is stretching.</strong> 2v = (6, 8) doubles every component: same direction, twice the size. −v flips the arrow through the origin. Any vector can be written as a combination of the basis vectors e₁ = (1, 0) and e₂ = (0, 1): (3, 4) = 3e₁ + 4e₂. That innocent identity is the seed of the whole subject — coordinates are just "how much of each basis direction".</li>
    <li><strong>Subtraction is the connector.</strong> B − A is the vector that takes you from A to B. If a drone sits at A = (−1, 2) and then at B = (4, −1), its displacement is B − A = (5, −3), regardless of where the origin is. Navigation software computes exactly this millions of times a second.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a vector and a point are the same thing because both are
    written as coordinate pairs. They are not. A point is a location; a vector is a
    displacement with no fixed home. The vector (3, 4) can start anywhere — drawing it
    "from the origin" is a convention, not a property. This is why adding a point to a point
    is meaningless while subtracting two points is perfectly fine: the difference of two
    locations is a genuine displacement.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="true or false — points and displacements" widget="judge" config={vmPointJudge} />

  <h2>Magnitude and direction (E7.3)</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>magnitude</strong> (length) of v = (x, y) is |v| = √(x² + y²) — Pythagoras
    applied to the components, since the vector is the hypotenuse of the right triangle its
    components make. For v = (3, 4): |v| = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5. For u = (1, −2):
    |u| = √(1 + 4) = √5 ≈ 2.24. Dividing a vector by its own magnitude gives a
    <strong>unit vector</strong> — same direction, length exactly 1: v/|v| = (3/5, 4/5). Unit
    vectors are how you express "direction only", and any vector can be decomposed as
    (magnitude) × (unit vector): 5 × (3/5, 4/5) rebuilds v. This split is used constantly in
    physics: a force of 50 N along the direction (3, 4) is 50 × (3/5, 4/5) = (30, 40) N
    componentwise.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="call the magnitude" widget="estimate" config={vmMagEst} />
  <p>
    The <strong>dot product</strong> u · v = x₁x₂ + y₁y₂ converts two vectors into one number
    that measures alignment. For u = (1, −2) and v = (3, 4): u · v = 3 − 8 = −5. The sign
    tells the story: positive means an acute angle between them, negative an obtuse angle,
    and exactly zero means perpendicular — test (1, 2) against (−2, 1): −2 + 2 = 0, so they
    are at right angles, no trigonometry needed. When you want the actual angle, the identity
    u · v = |u| |v| cos θ gives it: cos θ = −5 / (√5 × 5) ≈ −0.447, so θ ≈ 116.6°. In
    mechanics, work = force · displacement is this same product; in machine learning,
    similarity between two documents is usually a (normalised) dot product.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="fill the blanks — reading the dot product's sign" widget="cloze" config={vmDotCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "multiplying two vectors should give another vector — the
  dot product is just an odd kind of multiplication." Every multiplication you have met so far
  outputs more of the same kind of thing, so a single-number output looks like a
  mistake. It is not an oversight but the point: the dot product is a measuring instrument,
  compressing two vectors into one number that reports their alignment — how much of one points
  along the other. Positive means an acute angle, zero means perpendicular, negative means
  obtuse. Test (1, 2) against (−2, 1): the sum of products is
  −2 + 2 = 0, so they are at right angles — no trigonometry, one line of
  arithmetic.</p>

  <h2>Vector geometry (E7.4)</h2>
  <p>
    Vector geometry proves facts about figures using component arithmetic instead of
    auxiliary lines. Three tools do most of the work:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Position vectors.</strong> Fix an origin O; then any point P has position vector OP. The midpoint M of AB satisfies OM = ½(OA + OB) — average the endpoints' position vectors. For A = (1, 3), B = (5, −1): OM = ½((6, 2)) = (3, 1). You can verify instantly that |MA| = |MB|.</li>
    <li><strong>Parallelism as scalar multiples.</strong> Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the other. To show three points are collinear, show that AB and AC are multiples of each other. Example: P = (1, 1), Q = (3, 2), R = (7, 4). PQ = (2, 1); QR = (4, 2) = 2(2, 1). Since QR = 2·PQ and they share the point Q, all three lie on one line.</li>
    <li><strong>The parallelogram law.</strong> In any parallelogram OACB (with OA and OB as sides), the diagonals satisfy OA + OB = OC and, usefully, |OA + OB|² + |OA − OB|² = 2|OA|² + 2|OB|². Expanding both sides with dot products proves it in three lines — and it is the reason "sum of two vectors" and "difference of two vectors" are the two diagonals of the parallelogram you draw in physics lessons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A worked proof in this style: show that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each
    other. Let the vertices be O, A, B and A + B. One diagonal runs from O to A + B, the
    other from A to B. The first diagonal's midpoint is ½(A + B). The second's is
    ½(A + (B)) = ½(A + B). The same point — so they bisect each other. Four symbols, no
    diagram required, and the proof works for parallelograms in 3D or 10D just as well.
    That dimension-independence is the quiet superpower of the method.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the skipped check" widget="spot-error" config={vmCollinearSpot} />
  <Widget no="7" caption="two ways to bisect a parallelogram — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={vmDiagCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> vectors as objects — arithmetic with meanings, magnitude by
    Pythagoras, alignment by the dot product — and geometry turned into component
    proofs that never draw a diagram. Next, the verbs: rules that move the whole plane,
    almost all of them matrices.
  </p>

  <h2>Transformations of the plane (E7.1)</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>transformation</strong> is a rule assigning to every point of the plane another
    point. The syllabus family — reflections, rotations, enlargements, translations, and the
    shear you meet in graphics — splits into two camps. <strong>Translations</strong> slide
    everything by a fixed vector: P ↦ P + t. They are not "linear" in the technical sense
    (they move the origin), which is why computer graphics represent them with a trick
    (homogeneous coordinates) rather than a 2×2 matrix. Everything else in the family is
    <strong>linear</strong>: it fixes the origin and sends straight lines to straight lines,
    and every linear transformation of the plane is exactly one 2×2 matrix.
  </p>
  <p>
    The dictionary works like this: the matrix's <strong>columns are the images of the basis
    vectors</strong>. The rotation by 90° anticlockwise sends e₁ = (1, 0) to (0, 1) and
    e₂ = (0, 1) to (−1, 0), so its matrix is
    R = [ 0 −1 ; 1 0 ] (written row by row). A reflection in the x-axis sends (1, 0) to
    (1, 0) and (0, 1) to (0, −1): matrix [ 1 0 ; 0 −1 ]. An enlargement by scale factor k is
    [ k 0 ; 0 k ]. A shear that slides rows proportionally to height is [ 1 1 ; 0 1 ].
    Once the columns are known, the image of <em>any</em> vector follows by one
    multiplication, because linearity lets you distribute: M(3, 2) = 3·M(e₁) + 2·M(e₂).
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each transformation with its matrix" widget="match" config={vmMatrixMatch} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="composing rotations with matrix multiplication — watch a quarter-turn matrix act, then compose with itself" widget="equationStep" config={rotCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the first two steps evaluate the matrix on the basis
    vectors alone — east to north, north to west — and the plane is already determined; step 4
    gets (3, 2) for free from the same columns — the content of linearity. Then
    watch steps 5 and 6: applying the quarter-turn twice is a half-turn, four times is the
    identity, so the inverse is the third power. Matrix multiplication is motion composition,
    and the algebra of powers is the geometry of repeated turns.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Composition is matrix multiplication.</strong> Apply M then N and the combined
    transformation has matrix N·M — note the order: the one applied second stands on the
    left, because it must act on the already-transformed vector. And matrix multiplication
    is <em>not</em> commutative, which is no mystery: rotate the plane 90° then reflect in
    the x-axis, and you end somewhere different from reflect-then-rotate. Try it on the
    point (1, 0): rotation then reflection gives (0, −1); reflection then rotation gives
    (0, 1). The matrices [ 0 1 ; −1 0 ] and [ 0 −1 ; −1 0 ] genuinely differ, and geometry
    told us so before the algebra did.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="does order matter?" widget="branch" config={vmOrderBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>The determinant is the area scale factor.</strong> For M = [ a b ; c d ],
    det M = ad − bc. An enlargement by 3 has det = 9 — areas grow by the square of the
    length factor, exactly as the similar-shapes rules say. A reflection has det = −1: same
    area, but the <em>sign</em> records that orientation flipped, mirror-style. For
    M = [ 3 1 ; 1 2 ]: det = 6 − 1 = 5, so any shape's area is multiplied by 5. When
    det M = 0 the transformation crushes the entire plane onto a single line — every bit of
    two-dimensional information cannot survive, which is why no inverse exists and why
    det = 0 is the precise algebraic signal that a system of equations has either no
    solution or infinitely many.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drop the marker — the area scale factor" widget="place" config={vmDetPlace} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> matrix multiplication "should" work like number
    multiplication, so MN = NM. Almost no matrices commute. Multiplication is associative
    (you can regroup brackets: (AB)C = A(BC)) and distributive, but order is sacred, because
    multiplication <em>is</em> composition of motions and motions do not commute. The
    exception worth remembering: a matrix commutes with its own powers, and R⁴ = I for the
    quarter-turn.
  </p>

  <h2>Solving systems: the inverse at work</h2>
  <p>
    Solving Mx = b asks the reverse question: <em>which input produces this output?</em> If
    det M ≠ 0, the answer is x = M⁻¹b, with M⁻¹ = (1/det M) [ d −b ; −c a ]. Solve
    3x + y = 7 and x + 2y = 4: here M = [ 3 1 ; 1 2 ], det M = 5, so
    M⁻¹ = (1/5)[ 2 −1 ; −1 3 ]. Then
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the inverse-matrix solve" widget="order" config={vmSolveOrder} />
  <p>
    x = M⁻¹b = (1/5) [ 2·7 − 1·4 ; −1·7 + 3·4 ] = (1/5)[ 10 ; 5 ] = (2, 1).
  </p>
  <p>
    Check: 3(2) + 1 = 7 ✓ and 2 + 2(1) = 4 ✓. The geometric reading: the two equations are
    two lines; the solution is their crossing point, and the matrix view finds it by
    un-transforming the output rather than by trial elimination. Determinant 5 also promised
    the lines are not parallel (parallel lines would give det = 0) — the algebra and the
    picture agree, as they always must.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the pipeline is assembled — vectors in, matrix verbs acting,
    composition by multiplication, areas scaled by the determinant, systems solved by the
    inverse. The deeper section points this machinery at directions a matrix cannot turn,
    and where linearity breaks.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: state the parallelogram law identity, and say what the sum and difference of two vectors represent in the parallelogram's picture.</li>
    <li>Application: solve the system x + y = 5, x − y = 1 by the inverse-matrix method — write the matrix, compute its determinant and inverse, then check your answer in both equations.</li>
    <li>Why does det M = 0 mean a system has either no solution or infinitely many — and never exactly one?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The identity is: the squared length of the sum plus the
    squared length of the difference equals twice the squared length of each side. In the
    picture, the sum and the difference are the parallelogram's two diagonals. (2) The matrix is [ 1 1 ;
    1 −1 ], determinant (1)(−1) − (1)(1) = −2, so the inverse is (1/−2)[ −1 −1 ; −1 1 ].
    Multiplying by (5, 1) gives (1/−2)(−6, −4) = (3, 2). Check: 3 + 2 = 5 and 3 − 2 = 1. The
    determinant −2 also promised the lines are not parallel. (3) A zero determinant means the transformation crushes the whole plane
    onto a single line, so two dimensions of information cannot survive. A target off that line
    is unreachable — no solution; a target on it is hit by every point crushing to the
    same spot — infinitely many. Exactly one input per output is what a nonzero
    determinant guarantees.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A vector is a list of numbers; a matrix is a machine that eats vectors and outputs vectors — straight-line motion only, never bending.</li>
    <li>Addition is walking, scalar multiplication is stretching, subtraction is the connector from A to B.</li>
    <li>Magnitude is Pythagoras on the components; the dot product measures alignment, and zero means perpendicular.</li>
    <li>Midpoints, collinearity and the parallelogram law become component proofs that work in any dimension.</li>
    <li>A matrix's columns are the images of the basis vectors; composition is matrix multiplication, and the order is not commutative.</li>
    <li>The determinant is the area scale factor — negative sign records an orientation flip — and det M = 0 means no inverse exists.</li>
    <li>M⁻¹b solves Mx = b by un-transforming the output; algebra and picture must agree.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced lesson <em>Differential Equations</em> relies on this page's deepest
    idea: its superposition principle — solutions of linear equations add — is vector-space
    thinking with functions as the vectors. The advanced
    lesson <em>Logic &amp; Proof Techniques</em> formalises the proof style practised above:
    every claim earned by a checkable calculation.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Eigenvectors: the axes a matrix cannot turn.</strong> Solve Mv = λv and you have
    found a direction v that the transformation only stretches by λ, never rotates. For
    M = [ 3 1 ; 1 2 ], the characteristic equation det(M − λI) = (3 − λ)(2 − λ) − 1 = 0
    gives λ² − 5λ + 5 = 0, so λ = (5 ± √5)/2 ≈ 3.62 and 1.38, with eigenvector directions
    (1, 0.618) and (1, −1.618) respectively. Geometric
    meaning: the matrix stretches most along one diagonal and least along the other, and any
    repeated application of M soon points every vector along the dominant eigendirection —
    which is why powers of matrices model population growth, why PageRank is an eigenvector
    computation, and why principal component analysis finds the directions of greatest
    variance in data by finding the top eigenvectors of a covariance matrix.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Determinants beyond area.</strong> In n dimensions det M is the (oriented) volume
    scale factor, and it multiplies over composition: det(MN) = det M · det N — "volumes
    scale by each map in turn". That single identity explains why M is invertible precisely
    when det M ≠ 0 (a map that crushes volume to zero can never be undone) and underlies the
    change-of-variables factor in multivariable calculus, without which transformed integrals
    would be wrong by exactly that volume ratio.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the linearity ends.</strong> Not every transformation is a matrix: projection
    onto a line <em>is</em> (it is linear but loses information), but rotation about a point
    other than the origin is not (it moves the origin), and the map (x, y) ↦ (x, xy) is not
    because it bends lines. The expert-tier linear algebra lesson develops the full theory —
    vector spaces, rank, null spaces, the rank–nullity theorem — but the criterion is already
    visible here: a map is linear exactly when M(u + v) = Mu + Mv and M(kv) = kMv for all
    vectors and scalars. Test (x, y) ↦ (x, xy) with v = (1, 1) doubled: the outputs (1, 1)
    and (1, 2) do not double to the output of (2, 2) = (2, 4) — linearity fails at the first
    checkpoint.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions and frontiers.</strong> Multiplying two n×n matrices takes about
    n³ operations by the school method; the theoretical minimum (the exponent ω in the best
    possible algorithm) is one of the great open problems of computer science — the record
    has crept from 3 down toward 2.37 since 1969, and nobody knows whether ω = 2. Meanwhile
    the applied frontier is everywhere: quantum mechanics states are vectors and measurements
    are matrices; neural networks are nothing but long chains of matrix multiplications with
    simple non-linearities between them; and graphics engines transform every vertex of every
    frame through matrices in exactly the way this page introduced. Master the 2×2 case
    honestly and the 4×4 projection matrices of a game engine — and the million×million
    sparse matrices of a web search — become the same idea at different scales.
  </p>

